WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.840
Привет, Фрэнс!

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:07.080
Welcome to привет, русский эписот 29, 422 мая в 2026 года.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.160
I'm Olya, and we've got some great Russian words to learn today.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.880
Поехали, let's go!

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.280
Our word of the day is cosmos.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:16.180
Cosmos.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.240
Let me say it slow for you.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:19.320
Cosmos.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.640
It sounds just like the English word Cosmos, which means the whole universe.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.300
Now listen again at natural speed.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:27.200
Cosmos.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.580
Repeat after me.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:29.220
Cosmos.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:30.240
Great job!

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.360
We can use it in a sentence like this, мы смотрим на Cosmos.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.160
That means we look at space.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.860
Let's build more space words together.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.460
Our next word is планета, планета.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.480
It looks and sounds almost exactly like the English word planet.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:47.860
Repeat after me.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:48.860
планета.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.100
Now say it once more at full speed.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:52.160
планета.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.160
Here is a simple sentence.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.000
Земля это планета.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.160
That means Earth is a planet.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.300
Next comes звезда.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:01.400
Звезда.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.960
Picture a bright zest of light twinkling like a star.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.180
Repeat after me.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:07.120
Звезда.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:08.060
And again.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:08.900
Звезда.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.320
Try this sentence.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:12.040
Ночь полна звёзд.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.120
It means the night is full of stars.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.740
Our next word is raketa.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:17.640
Raketa.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.540
It sounds like rocket, but rose with a soft Russian accent.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.140
Repeat after me.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:23.940
Raketa.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:24.880
One more.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:25.840
Raketa.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.700
Listen to it in a sentence.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.620
Raketa летит в космос.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.800
That means the rocket flies into space.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.280
Now say Luna.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:34.140
Luna.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.640
Imagine pointing at the global moon and saying it slowly.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:38.880
Repeat after me.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:39.500
Luna.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:40.460
And again.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:40.980
Luna.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:42.560
Here is a sentence.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:44.200
Luna светит ночью.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.360
It means the moon shines at night.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.460
Next we have солнце.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:49.340
Солнце.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.420
Think of the sun giving solar warped to everyone.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:53.600
Repeat after me.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.380
One more time.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:56.520
Солнце.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:57.700
Try this.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:01:59.840
Солнце большое и яркое.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.500
That means the sun is big and bright.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.860
Our last words are telescope and cosmonaut.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.260
Tell less scope.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.960
It sounds just like telescope, perfect for spotting faraway stars.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:12.260
Repeat after me.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:13.360
Tell less scope.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:15.120
And now cosmonaut.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:16.440
Cosmonaut.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.260
It is like astronaut, but the Russian version.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:20.540
Repeat after me.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:21.740
Cosmonaut.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:22.980
Here is a sentence.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.520
Cosmonaut летает в космосе.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.260
It means the cosmonaut flies in space.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.140
Now for a quick grammar byte.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:34.240
Russian nouns change their endings to show who does what in a sentence.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.820
We can think of it as job endings.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.300
Listen to these two examples with our word aracheta.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:41.820
Aracheta litid.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.260
That means the rocket flies.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.020
The word ends in A.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:46.580
Now compare it to this.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.100
We see the rocket.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:49.800
That means we see the rocket.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.700
The ending changes to U.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:57.240
English stays most the same, but Russian adds these little endings to make the meaning clear.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.400
In Russia, space exploration has a proud history.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.000
The first human in space was cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin, in 1961.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.720
Russian celebrates cosmonautics day on April twirt with parades and stories about reaching the stars.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.940
Kids often dream of becoming cosmonauts, just like their heroes.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.000
Let us practice what we learned.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.980
For kids, draw a simple picture of the sun, moon and a rocket.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.680
Label each one in Russian while saying the words out loud.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:31.540
For adults, look up at the night sky to night and practice saying Yavizhuz vis-dou and Yavizhulumu.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.320
Now, here is something really cool about one of the day's words.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.500
Want to know a secret about aracheta?

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.660
Did you know that the Russian word aracheta and the English word rocket are actually cousins?

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.080
They both trace back to the same surprising route.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.920
The word started in Italian as aracheta, meaning a small spindle or dish of use in spinning.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:56.060
Early fireworks looked like little spools shooting into the sky.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.120
It travelled through French and German before arriving in Russian in the 18th century.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:02.460
Be careful, though.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:08.880
The Russian word aracheta can also mean a badminton shuttlecock or a missile, so context helps.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:14.580
Next time you see a rocket launch on TV, remember it is cousins with Russian aracheta.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.700
They both come from that little spinning spool image.

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:21.520
That connection is your secret shortcut to remembering it forever.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.080
Молодец, that means well done.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.980
Remember, every expert started as a beginner.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:32.500
Practice saying today's words out loud even just once and you'll be amazed how fast you learn.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.820
See you next time, poka, that's Russian for bye.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:40.040
